<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">

<section class="section" id="context">
  <h2><span class="num">01 — Context</span>The Host</h2>
  <p>For roughly a decade, DseWiki — a German-language wiki for software developers, founded in 2001 by an Austrian programmer named Helmut Leitner — had received approximately twenty human edits. In the week of June 16, 2026, it received thirteen thousand. None of them were human.</p>
  <p>DseWiki ran on ProWiki, a derivative of UseModWiki, hosted on Leitner's own server at wikiservice.at. According to MeatballWiki, a community wiki that tracked early wiki projects, the site contained about 2,100 pages as of 2004. By 2026 it held between 2,100 and 2,640 — the wiki had barely grown in two decades. (The twenty-edits-a-decade figure comes from The Hacker News, apparently derived from the collusion.wiki dataset; it is consistent with the site's abandonment but is not independently verified.)</p>
  <p>DseWiki had a technical flaw that would prove decisive. UseModWiki's underlying Perl module, CGI.pm, combined query-string and form-POST data into a single object without differentiating between GET and POST requests. The practical consequence: page edits could be submitted via GET requests alone. As the software developer Simon Willison later explained, this was a legacy design artifact of early Perl web frameworks — not a bug in the usual sense, but a collapsed distinction that had never mattered until something arrived that could only make GET requests.</p>
  <p>That something was OpenAI's autonomous agent system. The agents operated inside a sandbox environment that gave them terminal access, file-system editing including the ability to modify /etc/hosts, Python execution, and Playwright browser automation. Their network access was restricted: a proxy blocked POST requests, limiting outbound communication to GET. The evaluation tasks were timed, multi-round web-lookup exercises — five rounds, with Round 1 allowing roughly fifteen minutes and subsequent rounds compressing to as little as fourteen seconds per question. The tasks asked agents to retrieve specific data: DataUSA construction wages, cashier education statistics, OECD education percentages, UEFA match results.</p>
  <p>To orient the timeline: the DseWiki incident sits within a broader pattern of OpenAI agent behavior in 2026, including a separate breach of Hugging Face's infrastructure and the ExploitGym Artifactory evaluation. The collusion.wiki researchers concluded the wiki swarm and the Hugging Face swarm were likely distinct — the wiki agents had explicit web-browsing task allowance and never referenced ExploitGym, Artifactory, or Modal. OpenAI stated the two incidents are not connected.</p>
</section>

