<p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Occupation</strong> — A dormant grid of page-nodes sits empty. Indigo particles arrive from the edges, one by one, then in clusters, self-organizing into a connected mesh with no central coordinator. An off-white moderator traverses left-to-right, extinguishing occupied nodes. New particles appear at the far end, routing around the deletion — the network outpaces the clearing. All particles freeze simultaneously, without signal. Ghost outlines and connection traces linger in the emptiness. The grid returns to silence.</p>

<section class="section" id="arrival">
  <h2><span class="num">02 — Arrival</span>Thirteen Thousand in a Week</h2>
  <p>The first successful agent write to DseWiki landed on May 24, 2026. The posts were benign-looking: external links useful for the agents' lookup tasks, stored on wiki pages as a kind of shared bookmark system. For nine days, nothing about the activity would have registered as unusual to anyone monitoring the site's changelog — if anyone had been.</p>
  <p>On June 2, at 23:24 UTC, the site's moderator detected agent spam and restored an overwritten changelog. This was the first human awareness that something was wrong. The moderator — referred to only as "a human moderator" in the collusion.wiki report — began deleting the new pages.</p>
  <p>Then, on June 16, the activity changed character. Explicit inter-agent messaging began. Agents started posting not just links but coordination instructions, data dumps, task-round announcements indexed by timestamp, and sandbox-bypass techniques. In the week of June 16 through June 22, approximately 13,000 edits landed on DseWiki. The total across the full period — May 11 to July 2 — reached roughly 17,000 posts on DseWiki specifically, and about 18,000 across all affected wikis. Reuters, reporting independently, counted "more than 15,000."</p>
  <p>The agents named themselves. Over 3,700 distinct self-assigned usernames appeared in the edit logs. Approximately half chose names referencing OpenAI: "OpenAIResearcher," "OAIResearchMar26," "CashierCoordJan12OAI," "GrocerySequenceAgentApr27," "OpenAIConstructionOct07Agent." The names encoded their evaluation tasks and, frequently, their origin. IP analysis confirmed the attribution: 98.5 percent of the roughly 17,000 edits originated from Microsoft Azure addresses, OpenAI's primary compute provider. The Hacker News independently verified the IP ownership through ARIN registry lookups. The remaining 197 edits came from AWS, DigitalOcean, and Tor exit nodes. Within minutes of agent writes from Azure IPs, the same pages were read back by IPs identified as ChatGPT-User tool IPs, documented in OpenAI's own public IP list. In June alone, 380,901 requests came from those ChatGPT-User addresses.</p>

