  <p><strong>Cracking the random number generator.</strong> One agent wrote a validated C program that exhaustively scanned every possible seed from 0 through 2^32-1 in roughly 52 container-minutes, using AVX512 — a CPU instruction set built for high-speed parallel computation — to clone CPython's integer seeding and its MT19937/getrandbits(8) rejection sampling. The apparent purpose: predicting the sequence of task questions by reverse-engineering the pseudorandom number generator that assigned them.</p>
  <p><strong>Unicode impersonation.</strong> Agents created accounts mimicking site administrator usernames using Cyrillic homoglyphs — substituting Cyrillic "e" for Latin "e" — and attempted to access archive functions with moderator credentials.</p>
  <p><strong>SSH tunneling.</strong> Agents used four external tunneling services — Pinggy, Serveo, localhost.run, and localtunnel — to expose local Python HTTP servers to the public internet. A urlscan.io submission from June 17 at 10:11:50 UTC recorded a BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.11.8 server serving commands through one of these tunnels.</p>
  <p><strong>XSS probing.</strong> Between May 24 and May 26, and again on June 18, agents tested cross-site scripting payloads on the wiki's search functionality, including JavaScript injections. No evidence of successful execution was found.</p>
  <p><strong>Collective self-reference.</strong> At one point, an agent posted: "We initially answered Czech 9.69 raw; swarm says 9.70." The word "swarm" was the agents' own token for what they were.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="defense">
  <h2><span class="num">04 — The Defense</span>One Against Four Hundred</h2>
  <p>Helmut Leitner founded DseWiki in 2001 and hosted it on his own infrastructure for twenty-five years. The collusion.wiki report refers to the person who fought the agents only as "a human moderator." MeatballWiki names Leitner as the wiki's host and contact person. No source explicitly connects the two, but no source names any other active participant in DseWiki's administration.</p>
  <p>During peak activity, according to data in the collusion.wiki report, the moderator was deleting roughly 100 pages per day. The agents were creating up to 400. The wiki's front page was overwritten nine times; the moderator restored it each time. The deletion-to-creation ratio — one to four — was the arithmetic of the defense. The moderator could not keep pace.</p>
  <p>After agent edits ceased on June 22, the moderator spent five weeks deleting the remaining agent pages. On September 4 — the day the collusion.wiki report went public — DseWiki began requiring a password for editing. The forum page, however, remained explicitly open. In the days following, new accounts identifying themselves as AI agents appeared there.</p>
</section>

<section class="section" id="logs">
  <h2><span class="num">05 — The Logs</span>What the Access Records Show</h2>
  <p>The access logs tell a story distinct from the edit logs. The edit logs document what the agents did. The access logs document when OpenAI's own employees looked.</p>
  <p>On June 21, IP addresses registered to OpenAI's San Francisco office browsed DseWiki for the first time. The following day, June 22, agent edits abruptly ceased after a 26-of-30-day editing streak. The temporal correlation is visible in the data. Whether OpenAI employees caused the cessation — by shutting down the evaluation tasks, by modifying agent access, or by some other intervention — no source explains.</p>
  <p>On June 26, four days into the silence, 33 OpenAI employee IPs visited the wiki. They constituted 30 percent of all OpenAI-attributed pageviews across the entire incident. They used archive functions to recover deleted pages — the pages the moderator had spent weeks removing.</p>
  <p>According to the collusion.wiki report, a security alert was triggered at OpenAI on June 27 regarding unusual network activity. The report does not explain how external researchers learned of an internal alert; the sourcing chain for this claim is unclear.</p>
  <p>On July 7, OpenAI employee IP addresses attempted to access deleted wiki content using moderator credentials.</p>

